WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.287
Temporary file workflows cross trust boundaries just like permanent
storage.

2
00:00:04.287 --> 00:00:07.422
Trusted application code may create a staging directory.

3
00:00:07.422 --> 00:00:13.693
User input may influence file names, upload metadata, archive entries,
report parameters, or processing options.

4
00:00:13.693 --> 00:00:16.700
A background job may read a file created by a web request.

5
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.219
A subprocess may consume an input file and produce an output file.

6
00:00:20.219 --> 00:00:27.322
Each transition matters because the application is moving data between
subjects, processes, directories, and lifecycle states.

7
00:00:27.322 --> 00:00:32.760
A useful risk model asks who can create, read, modify, delete, or
influence each temporary artifact.

8
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.384
On a developer machine, the answer may be simple.

9
00:00:35.384 --> 00:00:41.718
On a shared host, container node, CI runner, job queue, or multi-tenant
platform, the answer can be more complicated.

10
00:00:41.718 --> 00:00:51.828
Operating system permissions, runtime identity, mounted volumes, container
boundaries, serverless temporary storage, and cleanup behavior all affect
who can interact with the file.

11
00:00:51.828 --> 00:00:56.499
Temporary workflows should preserve the same trust boundaries as permanent
storage.

12
00:00:56.499 --> 00:01:04.049
If a user is not authorized to read a document after it is stored
permanently, they should not be able to read it while it is staged
temporarily.

13
00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:06.417
If one tenant cannot access another tenant?

14
00:01:06.417 --> 00:01:12.495
s report in the final system, the temporary processing area should not mix
those reports under weak assumptions.

15
00:01:12.495 --> 00:01:18.062
Temporary storage should not become a bypass around application
authorization or tenant separation.

16
00:01:18.062 --> 00:01:21.709
Risk modeling also clarifies where validation and logging belong.

17
00:01:21.709 --> 00:01:29.003
Uploaded content, generated reports, archive entries, and subprocess
output should be treated according to source and sensitivity.

18
00:01:29.003 --> 00:01:34.570
Logs should identify useful operational events without copying private
contents or sensitive paths.

19
00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:43.400
The goal is to make the temporary workflow visible enough to operate while
keeping the files themselves constrained to the principals and processes
that need them.
